<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashidun Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rashidun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rashidun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> was the first Islamic caliphate, ruled by the four rightly guided caliphs Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali from the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> in 632 CE until 661 CE. It reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and the Byzantine provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Civil war after Uthman's assassination ended it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> succeeded it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate.jpg" alt="Rashidun Caliphate" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad adil at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_adil-Rashidun-empire-at-its-peak-close.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In late 639 Amr ibn al-As invaded for the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>, defeated the Roman army at Heliopolis in 640, and took the fortress of Babylon in 641.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>This disintegration left the empire without effective central authority just as the armies of the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b> invaded in 633 CE; the Battle of al-Qadisiyyah (c. 636 CE) destroyed the main Sasanian field army, and the last king, Yazdegerd III, fleeing eastward as province after province submitted, was…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Caliphate was established in 661 CE after the assassination of Ali ibn Abi Talib, the fourth of the <b>Rashidun</b> caliphs and the cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet Muhammad, when Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, the long-serving governor of Syria and the most powerful figure in the Islamic world,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ali died of his wounds two days later, which ended the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His death in 651, after successive defeats by the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>, marked the end of the last pre-Islamic Iranian empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebels besieging the house of Uthman ibn Affan, the third <b>Rashidun</b> caliph, who had refused their demand for a new caliph, set it on fire, broke in and killed him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-17">17 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
